  "final_response": "Done. The change:\n\n**`src/builtin.c:645`** — the unreserved set for `@uri` now matches RFC 3986 §2.3:\n```c\nconst char* p = CHARS_ALPHANUM \"-_.~\";\n```\nPreviously it was `\"-_.!~*'()\"` (RFC 2396's unreserved set, which also included the \"mark\" characters `!*'()`). Those four now get percent-encoded.\n\n**`tests/jq.test`** — updated the existing combined-format expectation (`'` → `%27`) and added two cases: a round-trip of the full unreserved set, and encoding of the reserved/other ASCII punctuation.\n\n**Docs** — `docs/content/manual/manual.yml` now cites RFC 3986 and lists the unreserved characters; I hand-applied the matching change to the checked-in `jq.1.prebuilt` using its existing roff escaping conventions (`\\-`, `\\.`). `tests/man.test` needs no regeneration — the `@uri` text lives in prose, not in an `examples:` block.\n\nOne thing I could not do: **I did not build or run the test suite.** This tree has no `configure` (only `configure.ac`), and `autoreconf -i` was denied by the permission layer, as was executing any locally compiled binary. So the new test expectations are verified by manual ASCII-code review of the changed code path, not by an actual run. If you grant approval for `autoreconf`/`make`, I'll build and run `make check` to confirm.\n\nNote this is a user-visible behavior change: `@uri` output for `!`, `*`, `'`, `(`, `)` differs from jq 1.6, so anything comparing against previously generated URLs will see `%21`, `%2A`, `%27`, `%28`, `%29` instead. That's the intent of the RFC 3986 alignment, but it's worth a NEWS mention — I left NEWS alone since it currently only has per-release sections with no unreleased entry to append to."
